ological, and gastrointestinal features", "Obesity and smoking as risk factors for metabolic disorders"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Metabolic Syndrome with Insulin Resistance (e.g., Type 2 Diabetes, Hyperglycemia with Complications)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.18038623213238192, "posterior": 0.20613524435068664, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Severe hyperglycemia in the context of obesity and smoking provides weak support for insulin resistance (B1/E), but the subacute presentation and rash suggest …"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Endocrine Neoplasms with Hyperglycemic Crisis (e.g., Glucagonoma, Insulinoma Crisis, Pancreatic Tumors)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.4701251833989096, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Rash and GI symptoms provide moderate support for glucagonoma (B2), but the subacute timeline and no tumor history reduce confidence."], "evidence_against": ["Absence of tumor history and subacute progression provide weak against glucagonoma (B2) as the primary diagnosis."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3", "B2.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Autoimmune / Inflammatory Syndrome with Secondary Hyperglycemia and Cutaneous Manifestations", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.011, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of immunological markers decisively excludes autoimmune/inflammatory syndromes (B3/D) as the primary explanation."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Infectious / Idiopathic Vasculitis with Systemic Involvement and Glucocorticoid-Induced Hyperglycemia", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.058, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Normal temperature and absence of infection markers provide strong against infectious vasculitis (B4) as the primary diagnosis."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive Metabolic Disturbance (e.g., Stress-Induced Hyperglycemia, Drug Reaction, Infection)", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.043, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus with Severe Hyperglycemia (Beta Cell Dysfunction and Insulin Resistance)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.85, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Atypical rash and GI symptoms provide weak against Type 2 Diabetes (B1.1) as the sole explanation."]}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Metabolic Syndrome with Hyperglycemia but No Diabetes (Insulin Resistance Without Beta Cell Failure)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.137, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Severe hyperglycemia with obesity supports Type 2 Diabetes (B1.1), but the rash and GI symptoms are atypical features that reduce certainty."]}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Drug-Induced Insulin Resistance (e.g., Corticosteroids, Antipsychotics)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.069, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of medication history provides weak against drug-induced insulin resistance (B1.3) as the primary mechanism."]}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Pancreatic Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2938282396243185, "posterior": 0.2938282396243185, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Beta Cell Tumor with Paradoxical Hyperglycemia (e.g., Insulinoma Crisis)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.14691411981215924, "posterior": 0.14691411981215924, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Adrenal Cortical Tumor (Hypercortisolism via Cushing's Syndrome)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.019588549308287902, "posterior": 0.019588549308287902, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2.4": {"id": "B2.4", "label": "Other Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors (e.g., VIPoma, Somatostatinoma)", "parent": "B2", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.009794274654143951, "posterior": 0.009794274654143951, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.3, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": ["glucagonoma", "insulinoma", "gastrinoma", "somatostatinoma"]}}, "frontier": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3", "B2.4"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could glucocorticoid-induced hyperglycemia explain the severe glucose elevation in this vasculitis candidate (B4/D), given the absence of documented infection?", "summary": "Hyperglycemia could suggest glucocorticoid use in vasculitis (B4), but lack of infection evidence reduces this possibility."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the cutaneous (painful erythematous rash) and gastrointestinal (diarrhea) features inconsistent with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (B1.1/A) as the primary explanation?", "summary": "Atypical rash and GI symptoms provide weak against Type 2 Diabetes (B1.1) as the sole explanation."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are there no documented medications in the case that would support drug-induced insulin resistance as the primary mechanism for hyperglycemia?", "summary": "Lack of medication history provides weak against drug-induced insulin resistance (B1.3) as the primary mechanism."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of documented tumor history and the subacute timeline (1 week) inconsistent with glucagonoma as the primary diagnosis?", "summary": "Absence of tumor history and subacute progression provide weak against glucagonoma (B2) as the primary diagnosis."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of fever (97.5°F) and lack of other infection markers rule out infectious vasculitis as the primary explanation for hyperglycemia and multisystem symptoms?", "summary": "Normal temperature and absence of infection markers provide strong against infectious vasculitis (B4) as the primary diagnosis."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of painful erythematous rash (necrolytic migratory erythema) and paraneoplastic enteropathy (diarrhea, abdominal pain) strongly support a beta cell tumor like glucagonoma as the …", "summary": "The painful erythematous rash with paraneoplastic enteropathy (necrolytic migratory erythema and GI symptoms) is classic for glucagonoma, a beta cell tumor (B2), providing weak support. However, the absence of documented tumor history and subacute timeline weakly contradicts this diagnosis. The ras…"}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B1", 0.478], ["B1.1", 0.239], ["B2", 0.194]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.309], ["B1", 0.18], ["B1.2", 0.117]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.47], ["B2.1", 0.294], ["B1", 0.206]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Severe hyperglycemia (>500 mg/dL) in a patient with multisystem symptoms (rash, abdominal pain, diarrhea) requires urgent metabolic stabilization to prevent acute complications like DKA/HHS.", "required_actions": ["Immediate blood glucose monitoring and management", "Assess for signs of DKA (ketones, electrolytes)", "Administer IV fluids and insulin if DKA/HHS criteria met"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading terminal branch (B2.1) has posterior < 0.75, dangerous alternatives (B1/B2) remain unresolved with posteriors >= 0.10, and tree has not reached max depth (2/3). Estimated remaining value of discriminators (0.89) justifies further exploration."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.89, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.2938282396243185, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of this patient’s condition?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Alpha cell tumor"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Beta cell destruction"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Beta cell tumor"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Hypercortisolism"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Insulin resistance"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Inappropriate absence of fever', 'Unexplained fevers', 'Beta-cell dysfunction'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B1", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.478}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2.1", "label": "Pancreatic Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma)", "posterior": 0.294, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 1}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 3, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.2", "B1.2", "B1.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=43%, source=both]\n\nPancreatic Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma) vs Adrenal Cortical Tumor (Hypercortisolism via Cushing's Syndrome):\n  Favours first:  abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n  Favours second: abnormality of the scrotum, abnormality of tibia morphology, aplasia/hypoplasia of the breasts, fatigue, hemivertebrae\n\nAdrenal Cortical Tumor (Hypercortisolism via Cushing's Syndrome) vs Other Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors (e.g., VIPoma, Somatostatinoma):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the scrotum, abnormality of tibia morphology, aplasia/hypoplasia of the breasts, fatigue, hemivertebrae\n  Favours second: abdominal situs inversus, abnormal atrial arrangement, abnormal axonemal organization of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal central microtubular pair morphology of respiratory motile cilia, abnormal ciliary motility\n\nRelated to Pancreatic Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma): epithelial neoplasm, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor, pancreatic non-functioning delta cell tumor\n\nRelated to Adrenal Cortical Tumor (Hypercortisolism via Cushing's Syndrome): benign reproductive system neoplasm\n\nRelated to Other Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors (e.g., VIPoma, Somatostatinoma): digestive system neuroendocrine tumor, grade 1/2, functional pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor, glucagonoma\n\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: Chronic rhinocerebral mucormycosis. > Pathophysiology\n  Patients with diabetes mellitus, particularly those with diabetic ketoacidosis, experience an increased incidence of infection. Hyperglycemia in individuals with diabetes compromises the immune system\n[RAG context for 'malaise' (score=0.68):]\n  Source: HIV, Antiretroviral Therapy and Metabolic Alterations: A Review. > History and Physical\n  Individuals with diabetes mellitus can be asymptomatic, with a diagnosis made only by laboratory testing. Hyperglycemia can cause polyuria, polydipsia, visual disturbances, and symptoms of sensory neu\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.69):]\n  Source: Chronic rhinocerebral mucormycosis. > Pathophysiology\n  Patients with diabetes mellitus, particularly those with diabetic ketoacidosis, experience an increased incidence of infection. Hyperglycemia in individuals with diabetes compromises the immune system\n[RAG context for 'and a painful rash' (score=0.72):]\n  Source: HIV, Antiretroviral Therapy and Metabolic Alterations: A Review. > History and Physical\n  Individuals with diabetes mellitus can be asymptomatic, with a diagnosis made only by laboratory testing. Hyperglycemia can cause polyuria, polydipsia, visual disturbances, and symptoms of sensory neu\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  painful rash → necrolytic migratory erythema → Pancreatic Alpha Cell Tumor (Glucagonoma)\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Investigate the presence of necrolytic migratory erythema (characteristic annular, erythematous, vesiculobullous lesions) as a hallmark of glucagonoma\n  abdominal pain and diarrhea → Verner-Morrison syndrome → Other Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors (e.g., VIPoma)\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess for Verner-Morrison syndrome features (e.g., hypokalemia, low gastric acid) to strongly suggest VIPoma\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
